# Frontend UI Development with shadcn/ui

## 1. Prioritize shadcn Components

Always prefer shadcn/ui components for UI implementations. These are pre-built, tested components that maintain consistency and accessibility across the application.

**Examples of shadcn components to leverage:**
- `Button`, `Card`, `Dialog`, `Drawer`, `Select`, `Input`, `Textarea`
- `Tabs`, `Accordion`, `Breadcrumb`, `Badge`, `Alert`
- `Form` (with react-hook-form integration)
- `Table`, `Pagination`, `Popover`, `Tooltip`
- `Sheet` (mobile-friendly side panel)

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Use shadcn components
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { Card } from "@/components/ui/card"
import { Input } from "@/components/ui/input"

export function MyForm() {
  return (
    <Card>
      <Input placeholder="Enter value" />
      <Button>Submit</Button>
    </Card>
  )
}
```

## 2. Never Modify shadcn Files

shadcn files are generated/imported components. Do not edit them. Instead:
- Override behavior through props
- Wrap components with custom components
- Use composition to extend functionality
- Leverage Tailwind CSS classes for styling

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Don't modify the shadcn Button component file
// (file: components/ui/button.tsx)
// export const Button = styled(button) => { ... /* your modifications */ }

// ✅ GOOD - Create a custom wrapper or use props
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"

export function CustomButton(props) {
  return <Button className="my-custom-class" {...props} />
}

// ✅ GOOD - Use existing props for styling
<Button variant="outline" size="lg" />
```

## 3. Emphasize Simplicity

Don't hack around shadcn components to achieve desired behaviors. Instead:
- First check if the component already supports what you need via props
- Propose simpler implementations using built-in shadcn features
- Combine multiple simple components rather than over-complicating one
- Use Tailwind utility classes for layout and spacing

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Hacking around the Button component
<Button onClick={() => {
  setOpen(true)
  setTimeout(() => doSomething(), 500)
  // ... complex state management
}}>Click me</Button>

// ✅ GOOD - Use a Dialog instead
import { Dialog, DialogTrigger, DialogContent } from "@/components/ui/dialog"

<Dialog>
  <DialogTrigger asChild>
    <Button>Click me</Button>
  </DialogTrigger>
  <DialogContent>
    {/* Your content */}
  </DialogContent>
</Dialog>

// ✅ GOOD - Consider Popover for lightweight overlays
import { Popover, PopoverTrigger, PopoverContent } from "@/components/ui/popover"

<Popover>
  <PopoverTrigger asChild>
    <Button>Click me</Button>
  </PopoverTrigger>
  <PopoverContent>
    {/* Your content */}
  </PopoverContent>
</Popover>
```

## Component Composition Pattern

Build features by combining simple shadcn components rather than extending one component with complex logic:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Compose from simple components
import { Card, CardContent, CardHeader, CardTitle } from "@/components/ui/card"
import { Button } from "@/components/ui/button"
import { Input } from "@/components/ui/input"
import { Label } from "@/components/ui/label"

export function UserFormCard() {
  return (
    <Card>
      <CardHeader>
        <CardTitle>User Information</CardTitle>
      </CardHeader>
      <CardContent>
        <Label>Email</Label>
        <Input type="email" placeholder="your@email.com" />
        <Button className="mt-4">Save</Button>
      </CardContent>
    </Card>
  )
}
```

## Styling Approach

Use Tailwind CSS for styling. shadcn components already use Tailwind and accept `className` props:

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Use Tailwind for customization
<Button className="w-full bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700">
  Full Width Button
</Button>

<Card className="bg-gradient-to-r from-slate-100 to-slate-200">
  <CardContent className="p-6">...</CardContent>
</Card>
```
